    "id": "madas/alevel-pure-a/q6",
    "question": "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-pure-a/q6-question-1.svg\" alt=\"The equation of a curve is given by the implicit relationship x y 2 + = x . x + 1 y + 1 ( ) Show that at the point on the curve with coordinates ( 1,1 , the gradient is 7 . ) 8\" style=\"--madas-width:584.0px\"></figure>",
    "steps": [
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-pure-a/q6-solution-1.svg\" alt=\"The curve is given by x/(x + 1) + y/(y + 1) = x2. Multiply both sides by (x + 1)(y + 1): x(y + 1) + y(x + 1) = x2(x + 1)(y + 1). [B1] clears both denominators Expand the brackets before collecting like terms: xy + x + xy + y = x2(xy + x + y + 1), 2xy + x + y = x3y + x3 + x2y + x2. [A1] expansion Bring every term containing y to the left: 2xy + y −x3y −x2y = x3 + x2 −x, y(2x + 1 −x3 −x2) = x3 + x2 −x.\" style=\"--madas-width:466.7px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-pure-a/q6-solution-2.svg\" alt=\"[M1] collects and factorises Write y′ = dy/dx. On differentiating the left side, the product rule gives the derivative of y times the bracket, plus y times its derivative. [A1] product rule y′(2x + 1 −x3 −x2) + y(2 −3x2 −2x) = 3x2 + 2x −1. [A1] implicit derivative [M1] differentiates the right side At (1, 1), this becomes y′(2 + 1 −1 −1) + (2 −3 −2) = 3 + 2 −1. [M1] substitutes the point\" style=\"--madas-width:678.7px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-pure-a/q6-solution-3.svg\" alt=\"Thus y′ −3 = 4, so the gradient is 7 . [A1] gradient Alternative using the quotient rule. Differentiate both fractions directly, remembering that y depends on x: + (y + 1)y′ −yy′ (x + 1) · 1 −x · 1 = 2x. (x + 1)2 (y + 1)2 The numerators simplify to 1 and y′, respectively: Hence 1/(x + 1)2 + y′/(y + 1)2 = 2x. At (1, 1), 1/4 + y′/4 = 2, so 1 + y′ = 8 and y′ = 7. Alternative using the expanded equation. Keep the terms on their original sides after expanding: Differentiate 2xy + x + y = x3y + x3 + x2y + x2 term by term: 2y + 2xy′ + 1 + y′ = 3x2y + x3y′ + 3x2 + 2xy + x2y′ + 2x.\" style=\"--madas-width:678.7px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-pure-a/q6-solution-4.svg\" alt=\"At (1, 1), 2 + 2y′ + 1 + y′ = 3 + y′ + 3 + 2 + y′ + 2. Collecting terms gives 3 + 3y′ = 10 + 2y′, so y′ = 7.\" style=\"--madas-width:472.0px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-pure-a/q6-solution-5.svg\" alt=\"Alternative using negative powers. In each numerator add and subtract 1: (x + 1) −1 + (y + 1) −1 = x2. x + 1 y + 1 The left side is [1 −1/(x + 1)] + [1 −1/(y + 1)]. Differentiate in the form dx{1 −(x + 1)−1} + d d dx{1 −(y + 1)−1} = 2x. The chain rule gives 0 + (x + 1)−2 + 0 + (y + 1)−2y′ = 2x. Thus 1/(x + 1)2 + y′/(y + 1)2 = 2x; at (1, 1), 1/4 + y′/4 = 2, 1 + y′ = 8, and y′ = 7.\" style=\"--madas-width:540.0px\"></figure>",
      "<p class=\"madas-total\">Total mark for question 6 is 8 marks.</p>"
    ],
    "promptText": "The equation of a curve is given by the implicit relationship x y 2 + = x . x + 1 y + 1 ( ) Show that at the point on the curve with coordinates ( 1,1 , the gradient is 7 . ) 8"
