      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-pure-a/q7-solution-2.svg\" alt=\"At y = 0, 12 sin θ = 0 gives sin θ = 0. Use 0, since π gives x = −1. Thus moving from x = 0 to x = 1 takes θ from π/2 to 0. [B1] limits in parameter order Since x = cos3 θ, dx/dθ = −3 cos2 θ sin θ. [A1] derivative Z 1 Z 0 Z 0 ydx 12 sin θ(−3 cos2 θ sin θ) dθ. A1 = y dx = dθ dθ = 0 π/2 π/2 [M1] substitution This is Z 0 Z π/2 cos2 θ sin2 θ dθ = 36 sin2 θ cos2 θ dθ . −36 π/2 0\" style=\"--madas-width:544.0px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-pure-a/q7-solution-3.svg\" alt=\"[A1] reverses the limits (b) The double-angle formula gives cos2 θ = (1 + cos 2θ)/2 and sin2 θ = (1 −cos 2θ)/2. [B1] double-angle identities Multiply as a difference of squares: cos2 θ sin2 θ =     4 cos2 2θ. 2 + 1 1 2 cos 2θ 1 2 −1 2 cos 2θ = 1 4 −1 [A1] product Now cos 4θ = 2 cos2 2θ −1, so cos2 2θ = (1 + cos 4θ)/2. [A1] power reduction Hence the product is 1 4 −1 8 −1 8 cos 4θ = 1 8(1 −cos 4θ) .\" style=\"--madas-width:545.3px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-pure-a/q7-solution-4.svg\" alt=\"[A1] required identity\" style=\"--madas-width:174.7px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-pure-a/q7-solution-5.svg\" alt=\"(c) Multiply the first-quadrant area by 4 for the whole curve: [B1] symmetry Z π/2 Z π/2 36 sin2 θ cos2 θ dθ = 4 36 A = 4 8 (1 −cos 4θ) dθ 0 0 Z π/2 iπ/2 h 18θ −9 = (18 −18 cos 4θ) dθ = 2 sin 4θ . 0 0 [A1] antiderivative Since sin 2π = sin 0 = 0, the result is (9π −0) −(0 −0) = 9π . [A1] exact area Source correction. Integrating cos 4θ gives 1 4 sin 4θ. The handwritten antiderivative retains a cosine.\" style=\"--madas-width:525.3px\"></figure>",
