    "question": "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-pure-a/q9-question-1.svg\" alt=\"2 f x ( ) = 3 x − 18 x + 21 , x∈ , x &gt; 4 . 2 ( ) a) Express f x in the form ( ( ) A x + B ) + C , where A , B and C are integers. 2 − 1 b) Find a simplified expression for f ( ) x , the inverse of f x . ( ) ( ) 3 − c) Determine the domain and range of 1 ( ) ( ) 3 f x .\" style=\"--madas-width:586.7px\"></figure>",
    "steps": [
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-pure-a/q9-solution-1.svg\" alt=\"(a) Completing the square Start from f(x) = 3x2 −18x + 21. Divide by 3 and complete the square: 3f(x) = x2 −6x + 7 1 = (x −3)2 −9 + 7. [B1] completes the square 3f(x) = (x −3)2 −2, giving Thus 1 f(x) = 3(x −3)2 −6 . [A1] constant term (b) Set y = 3(x −3)2 −6. Then y + 6 = 3(x −3)2, so (y + 6)/3 = (x −3)2.\" style=\"--madas-width:437.3px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-pure-a/q9-solution-2.svg\" alt=\"[B1] isolates the square Taking square roots gives q x −3 = ± (y + 6)/3. Since x &gt; 4, x −3 &gt; 0, so take the positive root. [A1] selects the branch p Hence x = 3 + (y + 6)/3. Interchanging variables yields q f−1(x) = 3 + (x + 6)/3 . [A1] inverse y x (4, −3) (c)\" style=\"--madas-width:472.0px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-pure-a/q9-solution-3.svg\" alt=\"The vertex of the full parabola is (3, −6). On the given domain x &gt; 4 we use the increasing branch to the right of the open endpoint (4, −3), since f(4) = 3(4 −3)2 −6 . = −3 [B1] boundary value Thus f has domain (4, ∞) and range (−3, ∞). Interchange the two intervals for the inverse: domain of f−1 = (−3, ∞), range of f−1 = (4, ∞) . [A1] inverse domain [A1] inverse range\" style=\"--madas-width:666.7px\"></figure>",
