    "id": "madas/alevel-pure-a/q10",
    "question": "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-pure-a/q10-question-1.svg\" alt=\"y f x ( ) x O The figure above shows part of the graph of the curve with equation cos x , x∈ . f x ( ) = 3 − sin x\" style=\"--madas-width:561.3px\"></figure><figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-pure-a/q10-question-2.svg\" alt=\"Use differentiation to show that 1 1 − 2 ≤ f x ( ) ≤ 2 . ( ) 9 4 4\" style=\"--madas-width:581.3px\"></figure>",
    "steps": [
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-pure-a/q10-solution-1.svg\" alt=\"Differentiate f(x) = cos x/(3 −sin x). Use the quotient rule, with numerator derivative −sin x and denominator derivative −cos x: f′(x) = (3 −sin x)(−sin x) −cos x(−cos x) . (3 −sin x)2 [B1] [A1] quotient rule with both derivatives Expand the numerator: Since sin2 x + cos2 x = 1, f′(x) = −3 sin x + sin2 x + cos2 x (3 −sin x)2 = 1 −3 sin x (3 −sin x)2 .\" style=\"--madas-width:594.7px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-pure-a/q10-solution-2.svg\" alt=\"[A1] simplifies numerator The denominator is always positive: For a stationary value, set 1 −3 sin x = 0. [A1] stationary condition Thus sin x = 1/3. [A1] stationary sine value Using the Pythagorean identity again, √ cos2 x = 1 −sin2 x, q q 1 −(1/3)2 = ± cos x = ± 8/9 = ±2 2/3. [A1] both signs Substitute each sign into f: √ √ √ √ √ √ 3 −1/3 = 2 2 2/3 2 2 −2 3 −1/3 = −2 2/3 2 2 9 −1 = 4 , 9 −1 = − 4 .\" style=\"--madas-width:557.3px\"></figure>",
